 ### [Uniform Grid Indexing](6941-uniform-grid-indexing.md)
-Uniform Grid Indexing is the standard row-major or column-major linearization of a dense 3D grid, serving as the baseline for all voxel indexing methods. It assumes a fixed, contiguous memory layout and provides $O(1)$ access time. It is the foundation for [Voxel Grid Indexing](1719-voxel-grid-indexing.md) but lacks the padding or coordinate transformation logic often required for robust boundary handling in rendering pipelines.
+Uniform Grid Indexing converts a 3D world position into a linear array index within a dense, axis-aligned voxel grid. It solves the problem of spatial lookup in volume rendering by mapping continuous coordinates to discrete storage locations using an axis-aligned bounding box (AABB) and fixed grid resolution. It sets itself apart from [Spatial Hashing](5002-spatial-hashing.md) by guaranteeing deterministic, collision-free indexing for all points within the grid bounds without hash collisions, and from [Sparse Voxel Grid](8021-sparse-voxel-grid.md) by using a fixed memory footprint regardless of data sparsity.
 
 ### [Sparse Voxel Grid](8021-sparse-voxel-grid.md)
-A sparser version of a dense grid that only saves non-zero elements as a list of coordinates and corresponding data. It requires additional indexing logic (e.g., hash maps or trees) to map coordinates to data, contrasting with the direct arithmetic indexing of dense grids.
+A sparse voxel grid is a volume representation that stores only non-zero elements as a list of coordinates and corresponding data, reducing memory usage compared to dense grids. It differs from its sibling [Uniform Grid Indexing](6941-uniform-grid-indexing.md) by avoiding storage of empty space, but requires accelerated data structures for efficient querying, unlike the direct indexing of uniform grids.
 
 ### [Memory Layout and Data Mapping](8133-memory-layout-and-data-mapping.md)
-Memory Layout and Data Mapping defines how voxel data is arranged in memory (e.g., row-major, column-major, or structure-of-arrays) to optimize cache coherence and GPU memory coalescing. It determines the stride values used in the linearization formula and is critical for performance in high-throughput rendering [G].
+Memory Layout and Data Mapping defines the mechanism for storing dense volumetric data in contiguous 1D arrays and mapping 3D voxel coordinates $(i, j, k)$ to linear indices. It solves the problem of efficient memory access and spatial traversal in volume rendering by establishing a deterministic row-major ordering where the $i$-index varies fastest, followed by $j$, then $k$. It differs from sparse representations like [Spatial Hashing](5002-spatial-hashing.md) by allocating memory for the entire bounding box regardless of data sparsity, and from [3D Texture Representation](2393-3d-texture-representation-cpu-gpu-indexing.md) by explicitly exposing the linear indexing logic for CPU-side or generic GPU buffer management rather than relying on hardware texture units.
 
 ### [Sparse Voxel Chunk Indexing](8606-sparse-voxel-chunk-indexing.md)
-Sparse Voxel Chunk Indexing manages memory by dividing the voxel space into fixed-size chunks (e.g., 16x16x16), each stored in a separate contiguous block. It uses a global index to locate the chunk and a local index to locate the voxel within the chunk, combining the efficiency of dense arrays with the sparsity support of hierarchical structures.
+Sparse Voxel Chunk Indexing is a spatial indexing method that organizes a sparse 3D voxel grid into fixed-size $8 \times 8 \times 8$ chunks to optimize memory usage and traversal speed. It solves the problem of storing and accessing large, sparse volumetric datasets by only allocating memory for occupied voxels while maintaining fast coordinate-to-index mapping. It differs from uniform grid indexing by using a bitmask to track occupancy within chunks, allowing for significant memory savings in sparse scenes, and from pure sparse voxel grids by grouping voxels into fixed blocks to reduce pointer overhead and improve cache locality.
 
